<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Das Kapital</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1867">1867</a>–<a href="/years/1894">1894</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Capital: A Critique of Political Economy, Capital</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Das Kapital, or Capital: A Critique of Political Economy, is a foundational text of Marxist theory by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a>, a critical analysis of the capitalist mode of production. Only the first of its three volumes appeared in Marx's lifetime, in 1867; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Friedrich Engels</a> completed the others from his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> and published them in 1885 and 1894. Its central argument is that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a> is driven by the exploitation of labour, whose unpaid work is the ultimate source of surplus value and profit; it sets out a labour theory of value and the idea of commodity fetishism. It became foundational to the international labour movement and to socialist and communist parties, and it remains much debated.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/das-kapital.jpg" alt="Das Kapital" width="330" height="556" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zentralbibliothek Zürich · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zentralbibliothek_Zürich_Das_Kapital_Marx_1867.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a> began intensive economic studies in the 1840s and, after the revolutions of 1848 and his exile, resumed systematic research in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1850. The writing of Capital then stretched over several decades. The work engages three traditions: classical political economy, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-smith">Adam Smith</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-ricardo">David Ricardo</a>, German idealist philosophy, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georg-wilhelm-friedrich-hegel">Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel</a>, and French socialist thought. Marx said his aim was to lay bare the economic law of motion of modern society, looking for the historical laws of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a>'s origins, development and decline. He delivered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of Volume I to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> publisher Otto Meissner in April 1867, and it appeared in September.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Volume I treats the process of production. It begins with the commodity, which has a use-value and an exchange-value, and argues that value is congealed abstract labour, measured by socially necessary labour time. Surplus value comes from the labourer working longer than is needed to reproduce the value of their labour power; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Marx</a> distinguishes absolute surplus value, gained by lengthening the working day, from relative surplus value, gained by cheapening wage goods.</p><p>Volume II, published by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Friedrich Engels</a> in 1885, examines the circulation of capital. Volume III, published by Engels in 1894, considers capitalist production as a whole, including how surplus value divides into profit, interest and rent, and the tendency of the rate of profit to fall. Running through the work are commodity fetishism, in which relations between people appear as relations between things, and the accumulation of capital, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Marx</a> argues creates a reserve army of unemployed labour.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The first volume was received quietly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Engels sent hostile pseudonymous reviews to newspapers to draw attention, and the first edition of 1,000 copies took four years to sell out. In the late 19th and early 20th centuries the book, often in simplified form, became a foundational text of workers' movements and socialist parties. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-bernard-shaw">George Bernard Shaw</a> studied it in 1883 and called it a revelation. It has also shaped sociology, political science and philosophy, and Marxian economics grew from it. Its labour theory of value, its predictions about <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a> and its association with 20th-century communist states have been criticised.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Das Kapital” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Charles Darwin’s On the Origin of Species (1859) undermined religious certainty and the idea of human uniqueness, and Karl Marx’s <b>Das Kapital</b> (1867) argued that capitalism was full of contradictions.</blockquote><a href="/movement/modernism">Modernism · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Karl Marx analysed the capitalist mode of production in <b>Das Kapital</b> (1867), and Friedrich Engels popularised the term.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/capitalism">Capitalism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote>Karl Marx, in <b>Das Kapital</b>, charged Bentham with taking the modern English shopkeeper as the normal man.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/utilitarianism">Utilitarianism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li></ul></section>
